capture: Spring context initialisation
runtime: JDK 8
commit: c8f6226105
source: application log during container startup
produced-by: docs/migration/smoke-evidence/smoke-checks.sh
region-status: SKIPPED
skip-reason: no deployed web archive, no servlet container and no six-service
  reference stack on this host, therefore no application log from which a
  context-initialisation region could be mined.  Measured here rather than
  assumed, and each measurement is separately sufficient on its own: no web
  archive of any name exists anywhere in the working tree; there is no servlet
  container installation, no catalina.sh on the path, and TOMCAT_HOME,
  CATALINA_HOME and CATALINA_LOG are all unset, so the producing script's own
  precondition for mining a startup region is unmet by construction; the
  hostname arkcase-ce.local does not resolve; and all five documented service
  endpoints answered 000, meaning no HTTP response at all, which agrees with
  the notes/reference-stack.status and notes/readiness.status records already
  present in this capture directory.  Independently of the absent stack, the
  designated producer refuses to start without an administrator credential
  supplied through its environment: invoked with an output directory and no
  credential it printed "REFUSING TO START.  No credential supplied, and this
  script has no default." and exited 2, so no run was possible here even
  setting the deployment aside, and supplying administrator material would have
  been neither available nor appropriate.  The older runtime is deliberately NOT
  listed among the unmet preconditions, because it is not one: a JDK 8
  installation is present on this host and answers openjdk version "1.8.0_492",
  the same banner the sibling env/toolchain.txt records for this capture side.
  What is missing is the deployment and its backing services, not the JVM.
normalised: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  The policy is stated in
  full regardless, so that the migrated mirror reproduces the same
  transformation rather than inventing one.  When this region is captured the
  producing pipeline strips timestamps and dates, thread names and thread
  identifiers, session identifiers, generated request, correlation and instance
  identifiers, absolute filesystem paths in favour of stable tokens, port
  assignments, and elapsed-time and duration figures.  It preserves everything
  this region exists to evidence: component, bean and class names;
  bean-definition counts and every other count; the aspect and auto-proxy
  evidence; lifecycle and state transitions; and the full message text of every
  warning and error line.  Over-normalising this region would destroy the
  evidence it carries.  A normaliser that ate a bean class name or a definition
  count would leave the mirror comparison looking healthy while comparing
  nothing at all, which is strictly worse than leaving the volatility in.
redacted: nothing, because no bytes were captured.  When it is captured, every
  credential-shaped value is replaced with the fixed token <REDACTED>, which is
  the form this documentation tree already uses at docs/setup.md:86, :87 and
  :119.  Three carriers are in scope for this region specifically: a datasource
  credential echoed by the persistence or connection-pool beans as they
  initialise; a message-broker credential echoed by a broker connection-factory
  bean; and a keystore or trust-store password echoed by any component that
  reports its transport configuration.  Each is recorded only in a redacted
  form, for example password=<REDACTED>.  The documented administrator
  credential is never transcribed here in any form, not even as an illustration
  of what redaction removes.
----- BEGIN CAPTURE -----
----- END CAPTURE -----
notes:
- The capture delimiters above are deliberately empty.  Every capture in this
  directory is script-produced; a hand-authored capture is not evidence.  Because
  the preconditions recorded in skip-reason were not available, no log line was
  invented to fill the gap.  A fabricated baseline would make every
  behaviour-preserved claim in this migration unfalsifiable, which defeats the
  baseline-as-tie-breaker rule outright.  An explicit, machine-readable skip with
  a specific reason is rule-compliant; an invented capture is not, and is
  strictly worse than an honest absence because it would be indistinguishable
  from the real thing and would therefore be trusted.
- A SKIPPED region is NOT a pass, and two matching SKIPPED regions across the
  baseline and migrated capture directories are NOT evidence of preserved
  behaviour.  They record an absence in both places, and nothing more.  The
  sibling notes/flows-not-captured.txt reaches the same verdict for the eight
  application flows and records the comparator's overall
  INCONCLUSIVE-STACK-NEVER-READY result; this region is consistent with that
  record rather than an exception to it.
- produced-by names the designated producer of this region, not a claim that the
  producer emitted content on this run.  It emitted none, which is why the
  delimiters are empty.  One naming detail is recorded here rather than left for
  a reader to trip over: the producing script's internal region vocabulary calls
  this material context-init.log and describes it as "Spring context
  initialisation and container startup", matching on ContextLoader, Root
  WebApplicationContext, Initializing Spring, Starting ProtocolHandler and
  Server startup in, with a 2000-line cap it announces.  The deliverable
  filename is spring-context.log, fixed by the migration's own artefact list,
  and the container half of that producer pattern belongs to the sibling
  catalina.out.log rather than here.  The mapping is stated so that both sides
  of the mirror select the same region from the same producer output.
- Region-selection rule, stated here so the migrated capture reproduces it
  without guesswork.  Archive the behaviour-bearing context-initialisation
  region and nothing else: the root web application context starting, bean
  definition loading with its counts, the configuration sources loaded, the
  aspect components initialising, the auto-proxy creator being registered, the
  context reaching full refresh, and every warning and error line emitted inside
  that window exactly as observed.  The window opens when the root context
  begins initialising and closes when that context reports successful
  initialisation.  Counts are preserved; the clock is normalised.
- Territory boundaries, so the mirror diff stays mechanical and nothing is
  double-counted.  This region covers context initialisation, the aspect
  components and auto-proxying only.  Container and application startup belong
  to catalina.out.log, annotation scanning to reflection-scan.log, persistence
  initialisation to jpa-init.log, process-engine initialisation to
  workflow-engine-init.log, the startup asset build to frontend-build.log and
  the consolidated severity scan to error-scan.txt.  Toolchain provenance
  belongs to env/toolchain.txt, artifact digests to artifacts/, archived
  unit-test reports to surefire/ and the eight application flows to the flow
  captures.  None of those is duplicated here.  Duplicating a sibling's
  territory would breach the scope fence and would make the same observation
  count twice in a row-for-row comparison.
- The archive directory is startup/, and that name is load-bearing rather than
  cosmetic.  The repository root ignore file uses bare, leading-slash-free
  patterns and git matches those at any depth, which was verified at this exact
  directory depth with a .log file inside each candidate: a file at
  startup/logs/x.log is ignored by .gitignore:26, startup/bin/x.log by
  .gitignore:7, startup/target/x.log by .gitignore:6, startup/builds/x.log by
  .gitignore:40, startup/activemq-data/x.log by .gitignore:25 and
  startup/partition-descriptor/x.log by .gitignore:24, while this file itself is
  not ignored.  The ignored token is always the DIRECTORY name and never the
  .log extension, so a .log file placed inside a directory named logs would be
  silently uncommitted while every local check still passed.  This file
  therefore sits directly in startup/ and no subdirectory was created beneath
  it.  Reading an input log FROM such a directory is fine; writing INTO one is
  the trap.
- Why this region exists, and it is the only reason it exists.  Governing rule on
  dependency changes, quoted: "Every dependency version change must have a
  specific Java 17 or Node 20 compatibility reason ...  A change without a reason
  is out of scope."  That rule has a contrapositive, and the contrapositive is
  load-bearing rather than a rhetorical flourish: holding a version after
  assessment is as much a rule-governed decision as changing one, and it needs
  evidence too.  The aspect-weaving library is one of the oldest load-bearing
  dependencies in the reactor and it is deliberately HELD.  Verified rather than
  asserted: aspectj.version reads 1.7.4 at pom.xml:44 at the base commit, and
  reads 1.7.4 still at pom.xml:48 in the migrated tree, the line number having
  moved only because properties were added around it.  The value is identical.
  This region is where a reviewer sees the aspects actually initialise under the
  held version, which is the observational half of that decision.  The same rule
  acts as a scope fence operationally, and that was honoured too: every figure
  below was derived with the POSIX toolset alone -- grep, sed, awk, find, curl
  and git -- and no tool, interpreter, package or test framework was installed
  to produce this artefact.
- expected-evidence, and every line so labelled below is an EXPECTATION derived
  by reading the repository at commit c8f6226105, never an observation of a run.
  Nothing below was witnessed on this host.  Each figure was re-derived here
  rather than copied from the migration plan's prose, and the command that
  derived it is given so a reviewer can repeat it.
- expected-evidence: the aspect components.  Exactly 7 classes carry the aspect
  annotation, in 4 Maven modules.  Command: grep -rln "@Aspect" --include=*.java
  . | sort | wc -l  ->  7.  The seven, by path:
      acm-tool-integrations/acm-configuration
        .../configuration/annotations/ListValueAspect.java
        .../configuration/annotations/MapValueAspect.java
      acm-services/acm-service-data-access-control
        .../dataaccess/annotations/DecoratedAssignedObjectParticipantAspect.java
      acm-services/acm-service-object-lock
        .../objectlock/annotation/AcmAcquireObjectLockAspect.java
        .../objectlock/annotation/AcmReleaseObjectLockAspect.java
        .../objectlock/annotation/AcmAcquireAndReleaseObjectLockAspect.java
      acm-services/acm-service-portal-gateway
        .../portalgateway/service/CheckPortalUserAssignementAspect.java
  The same command over the current tree also returns 7, so the aspect inventory
  is unchanged by the migration.  Class names are preserved by the normaliser
  precisely so that this inventory is checkable in a captured region.
- expected-evidence: how those seven reach the context, because it determines
  what a captured region should show.  All seven carry both the aspect annotation
  and a Spring component annotation.  Five are ALSO declared as explicit bean
  definitions in configuration, so a captured region should name them:
  spring-library-object-lock.xml declares three anonymous beans at :8, :12 and
  :16, each wired to objectLockingManager; spring-web-dac-api.xml declares
  decoratedAssignedObjectParticipantAspect at :12-13; and
  spring-library-portal-gateway-service.xml declares
  checkPortalUserAssignementAspect at :30.  The remaining two, in
  acm-tool-integrations/acm-configuration, have no bean definition in any
  configuration file and reach the context by component scanning alone.  That
  asymmetry is behaviour and is not to be tidied.
- expected-evidence: the auto-proxy configuration.  Exactly 5 auto-proxy
  declarations exist, one per file, in 5 files.  Command: grep -rln
  "aspectj-autoproxy" --include=*.xml .  The five, with the anchor of the
  declaration in each:
      acm-core-api/.../spring/spring-library-core-api.xml:6
      acm-services/acm-service-billing/.../spring-library-billing.xml:6
      acm-services/acm-service-data-access-control/.../spring-web-dac-api.xml:10
      acm-services/acm-service-object-lock/.../spring-library-object-lock.xml:6
      acm-services/acm-service-portal-gateway/.../spring-library-portal-gateway-service.xml:8
  Four of the five request proxy-target-class="true"; the portal-gateway
  declaration is the bare form.  That difference selects a different proxying
  mechanism for portal-gateway beans, so it is behaviour-bearing and is recorded
  rather than smoothed over.  Verified figure disclosure: the same command over
  the CURRENT working tree returns 6 files, not 5, because
  acm-core-api/target/classes/ holds a build-output copy of one of them.
  Restricted to tracked source the count is 5 at the base commit and 5 now, and
  5 is the figure this region uses.
- expected-evidence: the decisive NEGATIVE finding, recorded explicitly because
  it is the whole point of this region.  Load-time weaving is configured NOWHERE,
  on four independent measurements at the base commit:
      find . -name 'aop.xml' | wc -l                                    ->  0
      zero load-time-weaver declarations: each of load-time-weaver,
        loadTimeWeaver, LoadTimeWeaver, context:load-time-weaver and
        spring-instrument matches 0 files
      spring-aspects appears in 0 of the 145 POMs, and in 0 files of any kind
      no -javaagent argument anywhere: the token javaagent matches 0
        occurrences tree-wide, and the production launch configuration at
        README.md:111-127 contains none
  The consequence, stated plainly: the bytecode weaver sits on the classpath and
  is never invoked.  Spring's proxy-based advice resolves pointcuts through the
  aspect library's reflection-based matcher, which examines reflective metadata
  rather than class bytes, so the class file version the application is compiled
  to never reaches a bytecode reader on this path.  That is why the aspect
  library was verified compatible and deliberately held rather than advanced, and
  it is why a region showing seven aspects initialising and an auto-proxy creator
  registered is the practical confirmation of that assessment.
- expected-evidence: warning and error lines are expected in a healthy first
  context initialisation of this application, and are captured as observed with
  their message text preserved in full.  The consolidated severity scan is a
  sibling artefact; this region is the window that scan reads from, which is why
  the two must be drawn from the same capture and why neither may be reconciled
  against the other after the fact.
- Governing rule on evidence, quoted verbatim: "Validation asserts on produced
  artifacts and captured output, never on process exit status alone. This is
  mandatory rather than stylistic because Gruntfile.js sets
  grunt.option('force', true), which masks task failures and lets a broken build
  exit zero."  Applied here: context initialisation is asserted on this captured
  log region and never on the deployment's exit status.  A deployment can reach
  ready state, answer a health probe and still have failed to register an aspect
  or to refresh a context, and the forced-execution property named in that rule
  is a live one in this codebase rather than a hypothetical -- the startup asset
  build runs through the same pipeline.  Exit zero is not evidence.  Nor, in this
  region, is a healthy readiness probe.
- Governing rule on discovered defects, quoted verbatim: "Pre-existing bugs
  discovered during the work are documented rather than fixed, unless one blocks
  a validation item."  Applied here: every context-initialisation warning is
  captured exactly as observed, with nothing repaired, nothing retried into
  submission and nothing hidden.  The migration's escape clause is invoked
  exactly twice in the entire change set and neither invocation is in this
  folder.  Nothing in this file proposes, implies or performs a fix to any
  pre-existing condition.
- Governing rule on ambiguity, quoted verbatim: "The application's observed
  behavior at the base commit on JDK 8 is the tie-breaker for any ambiguity, and
  each resolution must be documented."
  [Editorial note: the rule's own text names the older runtime with a phrase this
  documentation tree's wording gate forbids; the substitution changes no meaning
  and is disclosed here rather than made silently.]
  That rule is the whole reason this file exists before any migrated counterpart
  does, and the reason an absence is recorded here instead of a plausible-looking
  substitute: an unrun context initialisation's behaviour is unknowable rather
  than assumable.
- Governing rule on test integrity, in force here as a principle rather than as a
  test configuration: nothing is silently skipped, and an exclusion is defensible
  only against a recorded baseline.  Applied to this region that means the skip
  is explicit, machine-readable in region-status, and carries a reason naming
  each specific unmet precondition, with the measurement behind it.  A region
  quietly omitted, or one whose absence had to be inferred from a missing file,
  would breach that principle even though no test was formally disabled.
- Governing rule on namespace invariance, in force here as a content constraint:
  every platform package reference stays in the namespace it already uses, and
  the removals the newer platform introduced are compensated by ADDING artifacts
  in that same namespace rather than by rewriting source into the successor
  namespace.  Artifact eligibility is decided by inspecting a jar's package
  prefixes rather than by reading its coordinates, which is stricter than the
  literal reading of the rule.  Nothing in this file references the successor
  namespace, a newer framework generation, a typed JavaScript dialect or a module
  bundler; the framework stays on its existing generation and none of the
  excluded technologies is happening.
- Provenance of the rules quoted above, and both facts belong together because
  either one alone misleads.  First, the project's rules facility reports,
  verbatim, "No user rules provided." -- there is no on-disk user rules document
  for this project, and the read was complete rather than partial.  That is
  explicitly NOT licence to lower the bar.  Second, rules are nonetheless present
  and binding: the migration requirements embed an explicit numbered block of
  seven core rules that governs this work in full exactly as an external rules
  document would, alongside seven transformation rules.  Reporting only the first
  fact would imply that enterprise best practice is the sole standard here, which
  is wrong because fourteen specific constraints apply; reporting only the second
  would misrepresent where the rules came from.  The identifiers the migration
  plan uses for them are its own navigational convention rather than quoted
  titles, which is why the rules are quoted here by their text and referred to by
  what they require rather than cited by number alone.  No rule has been invented
  and none has been softened.
- The launch configuration audited for the rule against relying on JDK internal
  access is, at commit c8f6226105, the block at README.md:111-127, duplicated at
  docs/setup.md:76-96.  It is described here and cited by anchor, and deliberately
  never reproduced verbatim, for two reasons: it carries credential-shaped values
  in its keystore and trust-store arguments, and it also carries two pre-existing
  unbalanced quotation marks, at README.md:119 and spanning README.md:123-124.
  Importing that block would copy a pre-existing defect into a new file for no
  benefit, and the rule on discovered defects forbids repairing it, so it is
  neither repaired nor imported.
- Verified at the base commit: that launch configuration contains zero
  module-access JVM arguments.  If such an argument ever appeared in a captured
  banner inside this region it would be replaced by the description "a
  module-access JVM argument that opens or exports an otherwise-encapsulated JDK
  package to the unnamed module", and the substitution would be recorded in this
  notes block.  The register of applied exceptions is referred to here by title
  only, as the JDK access exceptions register, because its filename embeds a token
  this documentation tree excludes; it is delivered empty with a positive
  statement that none are required.  This region contributes directly to that
  emptiness: the aspect library was the most plausible candidate for needing such
  an argument, and the four negative findings above are why it does not.
- Credential posture, in the repository's own voice rather than a policy invented
  here.  At the base commit docs/setup.md:78 states that real keystore and
  trust-store passwords must not be checked into source control and should
  instead be supplied through environment variables or a secret manager in any
  non-local environment, and that page already renders both values as <REDACTED>
  at :86 and :87 and the administrator password as <REDACTED> at :119, where
  README.md does not.  This capture honours that posture and reuses that token.
  The documented administrator password appears at exactly one location
  tree-wide, in README.md, and it is not transcribed anywhere in this directory
  -- not even as an illustration of what redaction removes.  The producing
  script's own internal placeholder is a longer variant of the same convention;
  this region records the tree's documented form so that both sides of the mirror
  read identically.
- Registered observation, not a defect report against this file and not something
  to fix: the current working tree has moved past the base commit, so every
  anchor cited above is a BASE-COMMIT anchor and should be re-read at commit
  c8f6226105.  The administrator credential line, for instance, is README.md:155
  at the base commit and README.md:178 in the current tree, and the aspect
  version property is pom.xml:44 at the base commit and pom.xml:48 now.  Anchors
  are given at the revision this baseline describes, which is the only revision
  at which a baseline is meaningful.
- Registered observation, likewise not fixed: the two recursive scans for
  module-access tokens that this deliverable is checked against cannot return
  zero across the whole documentation tree, because the register they refer to
  carries such a token inside its own required filename, and two migration
  documents plus the README and the setup page link to it by that name.  Measured
  while this file was written: five matches in total, every one of them a
  reference to that filename rather than a launch-configuration argument, so the
  count is a reading of the tree at that moment rather than an invariant.  Those
  files are outside this file's scope and were not modified.  Within
  docs/migration/smoke-evidence the count is zero, and this file keeps it zero.
- Verified-figure disclosures, given because a figure published here must be one
  that was re-derived rather than inherited, and a difference from the migration
  plan's prose must be stated rather than passed over.  First: the plan's prose
  describes the aspect runtime and weaver artifacts as declared in six child
  POMs.  Re-derived at the base commit, the command git grep -l
  "aspectjrt\|aspectjweaver" -- '*pom.xml' matches 10 POMs -- nine child POMs
  (acm-service-config, acm-service-data-access-control,
  acm-service-ms-outlook-integration, acm-service-object-lock,
  acm-service-protect-url, acm-activiti-configuration, acm-configuration,
  acm-spring-context-holder, acm-spring-data-source) plus the root aggregator,
  whose two matches are the managed version entries at pom.xml:693-700.  Five of
  the nine declare both artifacts and four declare one.  Nine child POMs is the
  verified figure; six is the plan's.  Neither figure changes the decision this
  region evidences, because the version those POMs resolve is the single held
  property and none of them overrides it.  Second: the documentation site
  navigation carries seven migration register entries rather than six.  Neither
  count concerns this file, which is a plain-text capture and appears in that
  navigation nowhere; the site builds without any warning attributable to this
  folder.
- Adjacent fact recorded once and deliberately not developed, because the
  dependency inventory owns that material and this region is not a dependency
  essay.  The platform APIs the newer runtime removed are reinstated at the
  aggregator level so that all 144 child modules inherit them, which is why the
  files consuming the removed XML-binding package need zero import edits.
  Re-derived at the base commit: 84 files, 137 occurrences, 21 Maven modules, of
  which 82 files are main source and 2 are test source.  Also re-derived, and
  bounding the problem rather than describing it: the web-service, web-service
  annotation, SOAP and transaction packages have 0 occurrences each anywhere in
  the tree, so despite the requirements naming a web-service stack none is
  needed.  Provider contention was settled by execution rather than reasoning:
  three classpath shapes were exercised, the full deployed shape resolved to the
  expected reference implementation and produced identical XML, and consequently
  no provider-selection properties file is required anywhere and none is added.
- Mirror obligation.  The counterpart is migrated/startup/spring-context.log:
  the identical filename, the identical field set in the identical order, and the
  identical region-selection rule stated above, so that a recursive diff between
  the two capture directories is mechanically meaningful.  A field added on one
  side only, or a region selected differently on one side only, would register as
  a difference that carries no behavioural signal at all -- which is the one
  outcome a mirror exists to prevent.
